--TEST--
`Core\Json::decodeAs<T>` reads a class only where its declaration says so, and the attribute decides both directions of the wire — `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list` and `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`, ADR 0063 R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`: a class participates in a wire format by carrying the
// attribute. These three declare the same two fields; only the first line of
// the first one differs, which is what makes the pair a bound rather than two
// examples.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Row {
    public int $n;
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(int $n, string $name) {
        $this->n = $n;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

final class Plain {
    public int $n;
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(int $n, string $name) {
        $this->n = $n;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

final class Legacy {
    public int $n;
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(int $n, string $name) {
        $this->n = $n;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

// § 2: the declared class decodes, and it is the constructor that built it.
bool $rowDecodes = true;
try {
    var $row = Core\Json::decodeAs<Row>("{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"ada\"}");
    echo "row-read=", $row->name, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $rowNoDecode) {
    $rowDecodes = false;
    echo $rowNoDecode->message, "\n";
}
bool $rowEncodes = true;
try {
    echo "row-wrote=", Core\Json::encode(new Row(1, "ada")), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $rowNoEncode) {
    $rowEncodes = false;
}

// The undeclared pair. The refusal quotes back the class it was *handed*, so
// the sentence is not a constant: two classes, two sentences, differing in the
// one place the caller chose.
bool $plainDecodes = true;
try {
    var $plain = Core\Json::decodeAs<Plain>("{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"ada\"}");
    echo "plain-read=", $plain->name, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $plainNoDecode) {
    $plainDecodes = false;
    echo $plainNoDecode->message, "\n";
}
bool $plainEncodes = true;
try {
    var $plainWritten = Core\Json::encode(new Plain(1, "ada"));
    echo "plain-wrote=", $plainWritten, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $plainNoEncode) {
    $plainEncodes = false;
}

bool $legacyDecodes = true;
try {
    var $legacy = Core\Json::decodeAs<Legacy>("{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"ada\"}");
    echo "legacy-read=", $legacy->name, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $legacyNoDecode) {
    $legacyDecodes = false;
    echo $legacyNoDecode->message, "\n";
}
bool $legacyEncodes = true;
try {
    var $legacyWritten = Core\Json::encode(new Legacy(1, "ada"));
    echo "legacy-wrote=", $legacyWritten, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $legacyNoEncode) {
    $legacyEncodes = false;
}

// One attribute decides both directions, so the two members have to *agree*
// per class rather than each be right on its own line: a decoder that grew a
// participation rule of its own — reading any class whose fields happen to be
// scalars — still answers plausibly above and fails here.
int $agreed = 0;
if ($rowEncodes == $rowDecodes) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($plainEncodes == $plainDecodes) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($legacyEncodes == $legacyDecodes) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
int $participating = 0;
if ($rowDecodes) {
    $participating = $participating + 1;
}
if ($plainDecodes) {
    $participating = $participating + 1;
}
if ($legacyDecodes) {
    $participating = $participating + 1;
}
echo "agreed=", $agreed, " of 3\n";
echo "participating=", $participating, " of 3\n";

// § 10's two branches: the class was named by the call site, so a class that
// cannot participate is a bug in the program rather than something the world
// did, and the refusal is a `LogicError`.
try {
    var $unreachable = Core\Json::decodeAs<Plain>("{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"ada\"}");
    echo "not refused\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $runtime) {
    echo "class=runtime\n";
} catch (LogicError $logic) {
    echo "class=logic\n";
}

// The codec is read before the document is, so one unreadable text is two
// different failures decided only by the class asked for: the class that
// cannot participate refuses first, and the one that can gets as far as the
// syntax. A decoder that parsed first would answer `ParseError` to both.
try {
    var $neverPlain = Core\Json::decodeAs<Plain>("{oops}");
    echo "not refused\n";
} catch (ParseError $parsePlain) {
    echo "malformed-plain=parse\n";
} catch (LogicError $logicPlain) {
    echo "malformed-plain=logic\n";
}
try {
    var $neverRow = Core\Json::decodeAs<Row>("{oops}");
    echo "not refused\n";
} catch (ParseError $parseRow) {
    echo "malformed-row=parse\n";
} catch (LogicError $logicRow) {
    echo "malformed-row=logic\n";
}
--EXPECT--
row-read=ada
row-wrote={"n":1,"name":"ada"}
Core\Json::decodeAs(): `Plain` has no JSON codec — a class participates by carrying `#[Json\Derive]`
Core\Json::decodeAs(): `Legacy` has no JSON codec — a class participates by carrying `#[Json\Derive]`
agreed=3 of 3
participating=1 of 3
class=logic
malformed-plain=logic
malformed-row=parse
